      "student_id": "3d999019-498e-4d72-a4c2-dc194c25948a",
      "student_name": "Smruti Pradhan",
      "submission_text": "B+ Tree indices maintain sorted keys with balanced height O(log N). When executing a nested loop join between Orders and Customers on customer_id, having a clustered index on Customers(customer_id) allows the inner loop to perform an index lookup in O(log M) instead of a full table scan O(M). For large datasets without indices, hash joins partition rows into memory buckets, which has O(N + M) cost but consumes more memory. Using EXPLAIN ANALYZE, we observe the scan method transitions from 'Seq Scan' to 'Index Scan', decreasing total buffer I/O by over 80%.",
      "submitted_at": "2026-08-31T14:20:00.000Z",
      "grade": 94,
      "feedback": "Outstanding explanation of B+ tree index lookups in inner loops and comparative analysis of hash join memory overhead. Well structured and precise.",
