    {
      "id": "sub-202",
      "assignment_id": "asg-102",
      "student_id": "3d999019-498e-4d72-a4c2-dc194c25948a",
      "student_name": "Smruti Pradhan",
      "submission_text": "To reverse a linked list iteratively in O(1) auxiliary space:\n```python\ndef reverse_list(head):\n    prev = None\n    curr = head\n    while curr:\n        nxt = curr.next\n        curr.next = prev\n        prev = curr\n        curr = nxt\n    return prev\n```\nFor cycle detection, Floyd's Tortoise and Hare algorithm uses two pointers: slow moves 1 step and fast moves 2 steps. If a cycle exists with length C and entry distance K, the relative distance between them decreases by 1 on each step, guaranteeing they meet within C iterations once both enter the loop. If fast reaches None, no cycle exists.",
      "submitted_at": "2026-09-01T16:45:00.000Z",
      "grade": 88,
      "feedback": "Great pointer reversal logic and Floyd proof.",
      "ai_suggested_grade": 92,
      "ai_suggested_feedback": "Excellent explanation of join algorithms with accurate complexity details.",
      "status": "graded",
      "graded_at": "2026-09-02T02:53:17.514Z",
      "graded_by": "teacher-123"
    },
